# Citation metadata for ETLIR. GitHub renders this as "Cite this repository", and
# Zenodo's GitHub integration reads it when archiving a release.
# At each release: set `version` and `date-released`, and add the Zenodo DOI under
# `identifiers`. Once the paper is published, add it as `preferred-citation`.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use ETLIR, please cite the specific release you used, as listed below.
type: software
title: "ETLIR: A Canonical Intermediate Representation for Traceable ETL Modernization"
version: 0.2.0
date-released: "2026-10-01"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23073564
abstract: >-
  ETLIR is an open-source framework for translating legacy ETL definitions into modern
  data platforms through a typed, versioned Canonical Intermediate Representation that
  decouples source adapters from target emitters, preserves source provenance, and
  reports target capability gaps explicitly. Its first reference pair translates a
  documented subset of Informatica PowerCenter XML to Apache Spark, with DuckDB SQL as a
  second target generated from the same Canonical IR.
authors:
  - family-names: Carimireddy
    given-names: Shiva Kumar Reddy
    email: shivacarimireddy@ieee.org
    affiliation: Independent Researcher
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23073564
    description: "ETLIR 0.2.0 (this release; cite it for results in results/v0.2.0)"
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23040381
    description: "All versions of ETLIR (resolves to the latest release)"
repository-code: "https://github.com/shiva-carimireddy/etlir"
url: "https://github.com/shiva-carimireddy/etlir"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - ETL
  - intermediate representation
  - data engineering
  - legacy modernization
  - provenance
  - Apache Spark
  - Informatica PowerCenter
  - DuckDB
